Recorded to witness the consistency with C of the sentence through which Possibility (pure) reaches the Axiom of Infinity at type $t$, and of that Axiom itself. The construction and its verification are in the write-up; no independent check is claimed. Interpretation of Σ, fixed for this record: each relational constant denotes the top element of its type and each individual constant denotes one fixed individual. Under it the signature schemata fail. Let $c\in\Sigma$ have relational type $	au$, so $c=	op_	au$ necessarily. Witnessed Possibility fails for the pure formula $x
e	op_	au$, witnessed by $ ot_	au$ but impossible of $c$; Separated Structure fails since $\lambda x\, .\,x$ and $\lambda x\, .\,	op_	au$ agree on $c$ and differ; Independence (signature Σ) fails since $c$ denotes what the closed pure term $	op_	au$ denotes; Distinctness (signature Σ) fails since $c=	op_	au$ is a true identity that C(Σ) does not prove. Everything that implies these fails with them. Further facts: the root proposition, true at the root alone, is an atom entailing every root truth, so Actuality holds. BF holds at the root: an entity of a relational type there is a family of component entities with a free root component, every component entity is the component of some root entity, so if $ orall y\, .\,\Box Xy$ holds at the root then every component of $X$ is universal and so is its root component, whence $\Box orall y\, .\,Xy$; BF holds at the one-world components trivially, so $\Box$BF holds. No Pure Contingency fails, since the pure sentence that there are at least two individuals is true at the root and false at the first component. Possibility (pure) fails, since Atomlessness is consistent with C but false at every world: each component is a one-world model and the root proposition is an atom. The type-t Strong Leibniz Biconditionals hold necessarily: at the root, the singleton of the arrow to component $n$ becomes that component’s identity under it and empty under every other arrow, and the root proposition becomes empty under every non-identity arrow, so every singleton is a strong world; the components are one-world models. Here the components are full Henkin models, so every set of arrows is a root proposition: the root propositions form a complete atomic algebra, giving Atomicity and Boolean Completeness at type t, and Relational Choice holds at the root by a functional choice in the free root component. Possibility+ (pure) fails: the component with three individuals has three at its only world; take two root individuals that differ at that component and agree everywhere else; the pure formula saying that $x
e y$ and there are exactly two individuals is consistent, but at the root there are infinitely many individuals, under every arrow into another component the two collapse, and under the arrow into that component there are three. □Relational Choice holds: Relational Choice holds at the root by the free root component and at every component, each being a full Henkin model. Distinctness-preserving collapse holds: let $a$ be the proposition true at the actual world alone (the identity arrow). For any true $p$, take $q:=a$ in the definition of $\Box_{
e}p$: under a non-identity arrow $i$, $\Diamond a$ would need an arrow $k$ with $k\circ i$ the identity, and none exists, since the components have no arrows to the root; under the identity, $p$ holds. So every truth is $\Box_{
e}$-necessary, although ND fails.

